# JavaScript formatting

Hand-written JS (`app.js` and any new `.js` files) follows this style. Do not reformat `data.js` — it is generated JSON from `generate_data.py` and must keep double quotes.

## Quotes

- Use single quotes for JS strings.
- Use single quotes for HTML attributes inside template literals.
- Keep a double quote only when it is the character being matched, e.g. `.replaceAll('"', '&quot;')`.

```javascript
// ❌ BAD
const id = "all"
el.className = "chip"
html = `<div class="bar">${label}</div>`

// ✅ GOOD
const id = 'all'
el.className = 'chip'
html = `<div class='bar'>${label}</div>`
```

## Semicolons and commas

- Do not use statement-ending semicolons (rely on ASI).
- Do not use trailing commas in arrays or objects.

```javascript
// ❌ BAD
const state = { filter: 'all', query: '', };
renderCards();

// ✅ GOOD
const state = { filter: 'all', query: '' }
renderCards()
```

## Other

- 2-space indent.
- Keep `function` declarations (do not convert existing functions to arrow functions unless asked).
